notes={
(1,2):'The stable reference is essential; feet may be shown together or slightly apart across conventions.',(1,3):'Study links also include supplementary planes and side terminology, rather than claiming every related definition is printed here.',(1,17):'Gliding as a movement component does not classify every example as a simple plane joint; sternoclavicular is more complex.',(1,28):'Rotation is a principal atlantoaxial movement, not proof that every part of the joint complex has no other component.',
(2,7):'The abbreviated source list should not exclude important facial arterial contributions.',(2,20):'Posterior arterial sources should not be equated with one historical Woodruff venous label; percentages are broad teaching estimates.',(2,23):'Regional lymph routes can overlap and include retropharyngeal/deep cervical pathways.',(2,27):'Mucociliary transport is central; nose-blowing suction is not a required sole mechanism. Sinuses do not generate voiced phonation, and proposed roles vary in evidential support.',(2,28):'Individual sinus-tooth relations vary; anatomical proximity alone does not diagnose symptoms.',(2,34):'Core ring groups include pharyngeal, tubal, palatine and lingual tissue. Tubal tonsil is associated with the tube opening, not synonymous with the adjacent pharyngeal recess.',
(3,8):'The historical Woodruff description is inconsistent in the literature. Distinguish the traditionally venous label from posterior arterial epistaxis pathways.',(3,9):'Anterior/posterior frequency and age statements are teaching generalizations, not individual diagnostic rules. A visible blood exit route does not precisely localize a source.',(3,16):'One blood-pressure reading does not prove the sole cause. HHT is a vascular disorder, not simply a platelet-count defect.',(3,17):'Wegener terminology is updated to granulomatosis with polyangiitis. The list is a differential, not diagnosis from nasal symptoms.',(3,18):'The list mixes different mechanisms. Polycythemia vera is myeloproliferative, while platelet and factor problems differ; medicines require clinician-directed decisions.',(3,19):'Age associations cannot establish a tumor, hypertension or a foreign body in an individual.',(3,20):'Severity and stability govern urgency; not every presentation needs identical tests or treatment.',(3,25):'Monitoring and medicines depend on clinical circumstances; antibiotics, oxygen and other measures are not universal fixed prescriptions.',(3,27):'Individual discharge instructions and an escalation plan take priority over an undifferentiated list.',(3,28):'Ongoing severe bleeding must not wait an arbitrary seventy-two hours. Specialist assessment guides escalation.',
(4,4):'A modified hinge/gliding description captures function better than treating condylar as a complete movement account.',(4,6):'Rotation is chiefly below the disc and translation chiefly above it, within coordinated movement.',(4,14):'One-line actions simplify coordinated, fiber-specific behavior.',(4,19):'Movement depends on coordination and fixation, not one muscle acting identically under all conditions.',(4,21):'The diagram explains mechanics, not a diagnostic test or a self-reduction technique.',
(5,3):'The length is an adult approximation, not an exact measurement for every person.',(5,5):'This is laryngeal voice differential material within a pharynx deck. Nodules and many polyps are benign; no single cause follows from hoarseness alone.',(5,9):'Boundary conventions near epiglottis/hyoid differ across descriptions; reference levels are approximate.',(5,11):'Hypopharynx includes lateral recesses and posterior subregions, not only a strip directly behind the larynx.',(5,19):'Specify V2 for major nasopharyngeal sensation and V3 for tensor veli palatini. Tensor is palatal; stylopharyngeus is the IX pharyngeal motor exception. A universal VII parasympathetic rule is incomplete.',
(6,4):'Arch contributions differ by region and tissue; second-arch tissue is largely overgrown. The tongue cannot be literally swallowed whole, but posterior position can affect the airway.',(6,6):'Palatoglossus is taught with vagal supply through the pharyngeal plexus rather than an unqualified independent XI motor rule.',(6,7):'Geniohyoid is a separate suprahyoid muscle with C1 fibers traveling with XII, not an intrinsic tongue muscle.',(6,8):'Transverse muscle does not itself divide the tongue as a connective-tissue septum. Its action narrows/elongates; vertical fibers flatten/broaden.',(6,10):'Papillae and taste buds are not identical; filiform lack buds in the standard classification.',(6,14):'Separate general sensation, taste and motor maps. Circumvallate papillae provide an IX exception despite their anterior-to-sulcus position.',(6,17):'Lengths and levels are reference approximations; do not force listed segment lengths into an exact universal total.',(6,18):'Three/four constriction schemes can group middle impressions differently. Source T3/T6 and incisor distances are not immutable; learn relations and sequence.',(6,19):'Velopharyngeal separation is the clearer term for nasal closure. Epiglottic inversion is one component of protection. Segment opening involves relaxation, traction and bolus forces; phase columns simplify overlapping events.'}
